        "statement": "Assume the inherited Axiom of Choice. Let $R$ be an irreducible simply-laced root system, normalize roots to squared length two, and write $\\theta=\\sum a_i\\alpha_i$, $h=1+\\sum a_i$. Let $\\ell>h$ be odd and $k=\\ell-h$. Put $C=\\{x:(x+\\rho,\\alpha_i)>0,\\ (x+\\rho,\\theta)<\\ell\\}$. For every integral $\\mu\\in C$ and every facet $F$ of its closure, there is an integral point $\\lambda$ in the relative interior of $F$ such that the ordinary Weyl-dominant representative $\\gamma$ of $\\mu-\\lambda$ satisfies $\\gamma\\in P^+$ and $(\\gamma,\\theta)\\le k+1$. Thus $\\gamma$ is in the dominant part of the closed shifted bottom alcove. A finite facet point $\\lambda$ need not be dominant before affine transport.\n\nThe assertion transports to every affine alcove. If the transported facet lies inside the strict shifted dominant chamber, the transported integral facet point is dominant. This is a geometric displacement assertion; it does not assert a tensor-summand or translation-functor conclusion.\n\nFor the finite coordinate verifications use the $E_8$ roots $\\pm e_i\\pm e_j$ and the half sums with an even number of minus signs, and simple vectors $\\alpha_1=(e_1-e_2-e_3-e_4-e_5-e_6-e_7+e_8)/2$, $\\alpha_2=e_1+e_2$, $\\alpha_3=e_2-e_1$, $\\alpha_j=e_{j-1}-e_{j-2}$ for $4\\le j\\le8$. The first six or seven give the $E_6,E_7$ subsystems.",
